WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:08.827
Hi, I'm Sohan. This is Procurement Approvals, a proof of concept I built on Microsoft Power Platform.

2
00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:14.077
It takes a purchase order from request to approval without one forwarded email.

3
00:00:14.091 --> 00:00:19.139
In a lot of companies, a purchase order still travels by email.

4
00:00:19.139 --> 00:00:25.398
It goes to the manager, then finance, then procurement, and nobody can see where it's stuck.

5
00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:30.554
Here's the app as Abdullah, an IT engineer who needs to buy something.

6
00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:37.132
He sees his own orders, what's waiting in approval, and the spend approved so far this year.

7
00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:43.162
He picks the vendor. Only vendors that procurement has approved are on this list.

8
00:00:43.162 --> 00:00:50.013
He enters sixty-one thousand eight hundred riyals, and the panel on the right shows the route straight away.

9
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:57.743
Three approvals, because it's over fifty thousand. A short justification, and he submits.

10
00:00:57.755 --> 00:01:06.369
A Power Automate flow takes over. It checks the order, opens the first approval step, and emails the manager.

11
00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:21.169
For the demo, I can act as each person. This is Khalid, the IT manager.

12
00:01:21.169 --> 00:01:28.821
He approves from the order itself, with a comment, and a second flow moves it on to finance within seconds.

13
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:34.478
Huda, the finance director, sees a budget check the flow wrote for her.

14
00:01:34.478 --> 00:01:39.917
How much IT has already spent this year, and where this order would take it.

15
00:01:41.328 --> 00:01:46.673
Turki in procurement signs off last, and the order is fully approved.

16
00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:52.644
A final flow emails Abdullah and books a processing slot on the next Saudi working day.

17
00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:59.193
Every step lands in the audit trail: who did what, and when, in Riyadh time.

18
00:01:59.203 --> 00:02:04.817
The vendor list keeps the commercial registration, the VAT number and a risk level.

19
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:08.603
High-risk vendors get a warning on every approval.

20
00:02:08.616 --> 00:02:13.920
Approvers aren't always at a desk, so the same app works on a phone.

21
00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:21.219
Under the hood it's six Dataverse tables, one canvas app and four flows. No custom code.

22
00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:27.609
Everything here is sample data in a developer environment, but the flow times you saw are real.

23
00:02:27.622 --> 00:02:36.153
The full build, the tests and the scripts are on my GitHub, and there's more on my portfolio. Thanks for watching.
